/// \defgroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d Update modules and their factories, part D (ReplaceObjectUpdate to WoundArrowUpdate)
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Sixteen of BFME 2's update modules, from ReplaceObjectUpdate to
///        WoundArrowUpdate in alphabetical order, seen through the factory
///        functions that create them: twelve module classes with their
///        instance factories and eleven module data classes with their data
///        factories.
///
/// Each module here is attached to an object by a `Behavior = <ModuleName>
/// ModuleTag_...` line inside an `Object` block in INI. This group covers
/// how a module and its data come into existence and which INI fields the
/// data accepts, read from the retail field tables. It does not describe
/// what the modules do every logic frame; where a class page says what a
/// module is for, it names the source (Zero Hour, BFME 1, or the field
/// names themselves). For most modules only one of the two factories is
/// documented here; the class pages name the other one where it has a real
/// name.
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory,
/// a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the registrations
/// are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`. All sixteen
/// module names covered here are spelled like their classes and are
/// registered as logic modules (module type 0).
/// - The **data factory** runs while INI loads.
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the data class's field
///   parse function.
/// - The **instance factory** runs when an object is built.
///   ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new object and the module
///   data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories walks through this flow
/// step by step; it applies here unchanged. Neither factory is called
/// directly: the module factory reaches them only through the registered
/// pointers, so no direct caller is known.
///
/// \par Interface masks
/// The registration's interface mask says which module interfaces the
/// module offers besides update. Eleven of the sixteen registrations
/// carry the update bit alone. The other five add one bit each; the names
/// below are the bits' names in Zero Hour's `Common/Module.h`, and this
/// page does not confirm what the bits mean in BFME 2:
/// - RubbleRiseUpdate: the die bit (\c MODULEINTERFACE_DIE);
/// - WallUpgradeUpdate: the damage bit (\c MODULEINTERFACE_DAMAGE);
/// - SpecialDisguiseUpdate: the create bit (\c MODULEINTERFACE_CREATE);
/// - ToppleUpdate: the collide bit (\c MODULEINTERFACE_COLLIDE), as in
///   Zero Hour, where ToppleUpdate also implements the collide interface;
/// - WeaponModeSpecialPowerUpdate: the special power bit
///   (\c MODULEINTERFACE_SPECIAL_POWER).
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from that
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory here allocates the module with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, runs the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data, and returns
///   the module as the Module base the factory stores. An exception frame
///   around the constructor frees the block if the constructor throws, as
///   a C++ new-expression requires; when the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - Every data factory here has the same frame, runs the data class's
///   default constructor, and then calls INI::initFromINIMultiProc outside
///   the exception frame, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not
///   free the new data.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Base classes
/// Read from the retail constructors the instance factories run:
/// - RespawnUpdate, RubbleRiseUpdate, SpecialEnemySenseUpdate,
///   StrafeAreaUpdate, TemporarilyDefectUpdate, ThreatFinderUpdate and
///   WallUpgradeUpdate each start by running UpdateModule's constructor
///   directly, with no special-ability base in between (WallUpgradeUpdate
///   and RubbleRiseUpdate also install vtables for further interfaces).
/// - ReplaceObjectUpdate, RousingSpeechUpdate, SpecialDisguiseUpdate and
///   WoundArrowUpdate each start by running the constructor that
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance runs for a plain
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate, so they derive from SpecialAbilityUpdate (a
///   structural inference from the retail bodies, the same one
///   \ref grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories makes).
/// - This page does not establish WeaponModeSpecialPowerUpdate's base
///   class.
///
/// \par Shared field tables
/// A data class's field parse function can register a base class's table
/// before its own, so one INI block accepts both sets of fields:
/// - ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData and RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData start
///   with SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, the shared
///   special-ability table described in
///   \ref grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories.
/// - SupplyCenterDockUpdateModuleData and
///   SupplyWarehouseDockUpdateModuleData start with
///   DockUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, which registers
///   \c NumberApproachPositions (an integer) and \c AllowsPassthrough (a
///   boolean), the same two fields as Zero Hour's DockUpdateModuleData.
/// - WeaponModeSpecialPowerUpdateModuleData starts with a shared base
///   table, and RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData adds a shared die-condition
///   table after its own; their class pages list them.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has StealthUpdate, SupplyCenterDockUpdate,
/// SupplyWarehouseDockUpdate and ToppleUpdate. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree
/// has sources for those four and for RespawnUpdate, RousingSpeechUpdate,
/// RubbleRiseUpdate, SpecialDisguiseUpdate, SpecialEnemySenseUpdate,
/// TemporarilyDefectUpdate, WallUpgradeUpdate,
/// WeaponModeSpecialPowerUpdate and WoundArrowUpdate, so those already
/// exist in BFME 1. ReplaceObjectUpdate, StrafeAreaUpdate and
/// ThreatFinderUpdate appear in neither reference. All INI field names on
/// these pages are read from the retail executable's own field tables.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their module tags and
///   every field listed on the data class pages. Several fields name other
///   definitions by text: FX lists (INI::parseFXList), object filters
///   (iniParseObjectFilter), sciences and special power templates. This
///   page does not establish whether a misspelled name is reported when
///   the INI loads.
/// - Hard-coded: the sixteen module names, their base classes, their
///   interface masks and what each class does with its fields. A new kind
///   of update needs a new class and a new ModuleFactory::init
///   registration (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Multiplayer: all of these are logic modules and their data comes from
///   `Object` blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Every duration field in the field lists on these pages (for example
/// \c StealthDelay, \c SpeechDuration, \c ScanInterval and
/// \c MinRubbleRiseDelay) is parsed by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt,
/// which turns milliseconds into whole logic frames once, when the INI is
/// read (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details).
/// \c RadarExtendTime, mentioned on the ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData page
/// only for comparison, is parsed by INI::parseDurationReal instead, which
/// keeps a fractional frame count. A constructor default for a duration
/// field is stored as it is, so it is a frame count, and a change to the
/// logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop) changes what those defaults mean in
/// seconds. BFME 2's frame-rate setup recomputes the parser's
/// milliseconds-to-frames scale from the logic rate, so INI values keep
/// their meaning in seconds when the rate is set before the INI is read;
/// data already parsed keeps the frame counts of the rate in force at that
/// time.
///
/// \see \ref sub_objects_modules, \ref sub_rts_model_flow,
///      \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories,
///      \ref grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories


/// \class ReplaceObjectUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "ReplaceObjectUpdate"; a
///        special-ability update configured with a replacement object, a
///        radius and an FX list.
///
/// Its constructor runs the SpecialAbilityUpdate base constructor and then
/// only installs the class's own vtables, so it adds no state of its own
/// at construction. Its data, ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData, adds a
/// replacement object, a radius, an FX list and a scatter flag to the
/// shared special-ability fields; the field names suggest an ability that
/// replaces objects within a radius, but this page does not describe what
/// is replaced or when. Neither Zero Hour nor BFME 1 has the class (Zero
/// Hour's ReplaceObjectUpgrade is an upgrade module with no established
/// relationship to it).
///
/// \see ReplaceObjectUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *ReplaceObjectUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ReplaceObjectUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line, shared with every other object of the
///                    template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see ReplaceObjectUpdate,
///      ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Module data for ReplaceObjectUpdate: the shared special-ability
///        fields plus what to replace and how.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. Its field parse
/// function, ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, first
/// registers SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse's table and
/// then its own.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Its own fields, read from the retail table:
/// - \c ReplaceObject, read by a dedicated parser this page does not
///   describe;
/// - \c ReplaceRadius, a real number;
/// - \c ReplaceFX, an FX list looked up by name;
/// - \c Scatter, a boolean.
///
/// \see ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ReplaceObjectUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs
/// ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when \p ini is
/// not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see ReplaceObjectUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ReplaceObjectUpdateModuleData


/// \class RespawnUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "RespawnUpdate"; it starts asleep.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, sets several of its
/// own fields to minus one or zero, and puts the module to
/// sleep indefinitely with UpdateModule::setWakeFrame, so it does no work
/// until something wakes it. Going by its name, the module is concerned
/// with bringing an object back after death; this page does not describe
/// how. Its data factory, RespawnUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
/// is not documented here. BFME 1 has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see RespawnUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *RespawnUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "RespawnUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by RespawnUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see RespawnUpdate


/// \class RousingSpeechUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "RousingSpeechUpdate"; a
///        special-ability update whose data describes a bonus for objects
///        around the speaker.
///
/// Its constructor runs the SpecialAbilityUpdate base constructor,
/// initialises one member object and clears its remaining state. The
/// data fields (RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData) give a bonus radius, a
/// speech duration and update interval, FX for the leader and for
/// followers, an optional wave, attribute modifier names, a level-up flag
/// and an object filter, which together describe an ability that
/// applies modifiers to filtered objects around the speaker; the order of
/// events is not described here. BFME 1 has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see RousingSpeechUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *RousingSpeechUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "RousingSpeechUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line, shared with every other object of the
///                    template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see RousingSpeechUpdate,
///      RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Module data for RousingSpeechUpdate: the shared special-ability
///        fields plus radius, timing, FX and modifier settings.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. Its field parse
/// function, RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, first
/// registers SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse's table and
/// then its own.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Its own fields, read from the retail table:
/// - \c BonusRadius and \c WaveWidth, real numbers;
/// - \c SpeechDuration and \c UpdateInterval, durations;
/// - \c LeaderFX and \c FollowerFX, FX lists looked up by name;
/// - \c CreateWave and \c LevelUp, booleans;
/// - \c ModifierName, a list of names that each line appends to
///   (INI::parseAsciiStringVectorAppend), so several lines add several
///   names;
/// - \c ObjectFilter, an object filter read by iniParseObjectFilter.
///
/// \see RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "RousingSpeechUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then,
/// when \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc
/// and RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see RousingSpeechUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      RousingSpeechUpdateModuleData


/// \class RubbleRiseUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "RubbleRiseUpdate" with the bit Zero
///        Hour uses for the die interface; it starts asleep.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, clears its own state
/// and puts the module to sleep indefinitely with
/// UpdateModule::setWakeFrame. Its registration adds the bit Zero Hour
/// uses for the die interface, and its data carries a die-condition table
/// (see RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData), which suggests that the object's
/// death is what wakes it; this page does not confirm that. BFME 1's
/// RubbleRiseUpdate has a per-phase effects step, which fits the
/// per-phase object creation lists and FX lists in the data; the phases
/// themselves are not described here. Zero Hour does not have the class.
///
/// \see RubbleRiseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *RubbleRiseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "RubbleRiseUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line, shared with every other object of the
///                    template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see RubbleRiseUpdate,
///      RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Module data for RubbleRiseUpdate: rise and burst timing, motion
///        settings, effect lists and the deaths that trigger it.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. Its field parse
/// function, RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, registers the
/// class's own table first and then a shared die-condition table, which
/// it places at an offset inside this data, so the conditions are a
/// member of the data rather than a base class.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Its own fields, read from the retail table:
/// - durations: \c MinRubbleRiseDelay, \c MaxRubbleRiseDelay,
///   \c MinBurstDelay and \c MaxBurstDelay;
/// - real numbers: \c RubbleRiseDamping, \c RubbleHeight and
///   \c MaxShudder;
/// - \c BigBurstFrequency, an integer;
/// - \c OCL and \c FXList, read by RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::parseOCL
///   and RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::parseFXList into lists the
///   constructor sets up four of each; the line syntax is not described
///   here.
///
/// The die-condition fields are \c DeathTypes (read by
/// INI::parseDeathTypeFlags), \c ExemptStatus and \c RequiredStatus (read
/// by a dedicated parser; Zero Hour reads them as object status flags),
/// \c DamageAmountRequired (a real
/// number) and \c MinKillerAngle and \c MaxKillerAngle (angles, read by
/// INI::parseAngleReal). Zero Hour's DieMuxData has \c DeathTypes,
/// \c ExemptStatus and \c RequiredStatus too, plus \c VeterancyLevels,
/// which this table does not have. In Zero Hour a die module ignores a
/// death unless its type and the object's veterancy level are listed, none
/// of the exempt status bits is set and, when required bits are given, all
/// of them are set.
///
/// \see RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "RubbleRiseUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when
/// \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see RubbleRiseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      RubbleRiseUpdateModuleData


/// \class SpecialDisguiseUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "SpecialDisguiseUpdate"; a
///        special-ability update that also carries the bit Zero Hour uses
///        for the create interface.
///
/// Its constructor runs the SpecialAbilityUpdate base constructor,
/// initialises one embedded member and clears a real-number field and a
/// flag. Its registration adds the bit Zero Hour uses for the create
/// interface; in Zero Hour that bit means the module is called when its
/// object is created. This page does not confirm the BFME 2 meaning or say
/// what the disguise does. Its data factory,
/// SpecialDisguiseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, is not documented
/// here. BFME 1 has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see SpecialDisguiseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *SpecialDisguiseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SpecialDisguiseUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by
///                    SpecialDisguiseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see SpecialDisguiseUpdate


/// \class SpecialEnemySenseUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "SpecialEnemySenseUpdate",
///        configured by an object filter, a scan range and a scan interval.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor and asks to be updated
/// on the next logic frame with UpdateModule::setWakeFrame; it adds no
/// state of its own. Its data (SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData) holds an
/// object filter, a scan range and a scan interval, which describe a scan
/// for matching objects nearby; what the module does with a match is not
/// described here. BFME 1 has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see SpecialEnemySenseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *SpecialEnemySenseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SpecialEnemySenseUpdate":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData for
///                    this module line, shared with every other object of
///                    the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see SpecialEnemySenseUpdate,
///      SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Module data for SpecialEnemySenseUpdate: which objects to sense,
///        how far and how often.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table:
/// - \c SpecialEnemyFilter, an object filter read by iniParseObjectFilter;
/// - \c ScanRange, a real number, 0 by default;
/// - \c ScanInterval, a duration. Its constructor default is 1, stored
///   without the millisecond conversion INI values get, so the default is
///   one logic frame (see the group page).
///
/// \see SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "SpecialEnemySenseUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then,
/// when \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc
/// and SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see SpecialEnemySenseUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      SpecialEnemySenseUpdateModuleData


/// \class StealthUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Module data for the "StealthUpdate" module: when an object may
///        stealth, how it looks while stealthed, its disguise settings, and
///        the One Ring, detection-range and upgrade-name settings that
///        Zero Hour's table lacks.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// StealthUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template. The module itself is created by
/// StealthUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, which is not documented here.
/// Zero Hour: StealthUpdate makes its object stealthed after a delay when
/// the forbidden conditions (such as moving or attacking) do not apply,
/// and handles disguises.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table:
/// - durations: \c StealthDelay, \c PulseFrequency,
///   \c DisguiseTransitionTime, \c DisguiseRevealTransitionTime,
///   \c OneRingDelayOn, \c OneRingDelayOff, \c RingAnimTimeOn,
///   \c RingAnimTimeOff and \c RingDelayAfterRemoving;
/// - \c MoveThresholdSpeed, a speed (INI::parseVelocityReal);
/// - \c StealthForbiddenConditions, any of these names, read by
///   INI::parseBitString32 from a list in the executable: \c ATTACKING,
///   \c MOVING, \c USING_ABILITY, \c FIRING_PRIMARY, \c FIRING_SECONDARY,
///   \c FIRING_TERTIARY, \c FIRING_QUATERNARY, \c FIRING_QUINARY,
///   \c MOUNTED, \c AWAY_FROM_TREES, \c HORDEBRAIN_NOT_STEALTHED,
///   \c PRIMARY_FORMATION and \c TAKING_DAMAGE;
/// - \c HintDetectableConditions, read by a dedicated parser (Zero Hour
///   reads this field as a set of object status flags);
/// - \c FriendlyOpacityMin and \c FriendlyOpacityMax, percentages;
/// - booleans: \c DisguisesAsTeam, \c OrderIdleEnemiesToAttackMeUponReveal,
///   \c StartsActive, \c InnateStealth and \c DetectedByFriendliesOnly;
/// - real numbers: \c RevealDistanceFromTarget and
///   \c DetectedByAnyoneRange;
/// - FX lists: \c DisguiseFX, \c DisguiseRevealFX, \c BecomeStealthedFX,
///   \c ExitStealthFX, \c BecomeStealthedOneRingFX and
///   \c ExitStealthOneRingFX;
/// - lists of names (INI::parseAsciiStringVector, so a later line replaces
///   an earlier one): \c RemoveTerrainRestrictionOnUpgrade,
///   \c RequiredUpgradeNames and \c ForbiddenUpgradeNames;
/// - read by dedicated parsers this page does not describe:
///   \c RevealWeaponSets, \c VoiceMoveToStealthyArea,
///   \c VoiceEnterStateMoveToStealthyArea, \c EvaEventDetectedEnemy,
///   \c EvaEventDetectedAlly and \c EvaEventDetectedOwner.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// Compared with Zero Hour's StealthUpdateModuleData table, BFME 2 keeps
/// \c StealthDelay, \c MoveThresholdSpeed, \c StealthForbiddenConditions,
/// the opacity, pulse and disguise fields, \c RevealDistanceFromTarget,
/// \c OrderIdleEnemiesToAttackMeUponReveal and \c InnateStealth with the
/// same parsers, and keeps \c HintDetectableConditions with a parser this
/// page does not compare. It drops Zero Hour's
/// \c RequiredStatus, \c ForbiddenStatus, \c UseRiderStealth,
/// \c EnemyDetectionEvaEvent, \c OwnDetectionEvaEvent,
/// \c BlackMarketCheckDelay and \c GrantedBySpecialPower, and adds the
/// rest of the fields above, including the One Ring timing and FX fields
/// and the upgrade-name lists. BFME 1's table already has the One Ring
/// fields, \c DetectedByAnyoneRange, \c DetectedByFriendliesOnly and
/// \c RemoveTerrainRestrictionOnUpgrade. BFME 2's condition-name list
/// keeps Zero Hour's first six names and \c TAKING_DAMAGE, drops
/// \c NO_BLACK_MARKET and \c RIDERS_ATTACKING, and adds the rest. The list is hard-coded: a mod
/// cannot add a condition name.
///
/// \see StealthUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *StealthUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "StealthUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs StealthUpdateModuleData
/// with its defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and StealthUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see StealthUpdateModuleData


/// \class StrafeAreaUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "StrafeAreaUpdate"; it fires a
