/// \class ThreatFinderUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Update module registered as "ThreatFinderUpdate", configured by
///        a single default radius.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, clears one field of
/// its own and installs the class's vtables. Its data
/// (ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData) holds only \c DefaultRadius. When
/// ThreatFinderUpdate::mapLoadPostProcess is given the object's map
/// properties, it creates a threat finder under a name copied from its
/// object. The finder is registered with the threat finder manager
/// (ThreatFinderManager::add) only when that name is not empty. The
/// finder's radius is the map object's \c objectThreatFinderRadius
/// property when the map sets it, and \c DefaultRadius otherwise; an
/// unnamed object still gets a finder and a radius, but the finder never
/// reaches the manager. The module's destructor deletes the finder, and
/// the finder's destructor removes it from the manager
/// (ThreatFinderManager::remove) under the same non-empty-name condition.
/// Map scripts read a finder by name through
/// ScriptActions::doSetCounterToThreatFinderThreat, which sets a script
/// counter to the threat ThreatFinder::getThreatForPlayer reports for a
/// player, with the threat type \c Enemies or \c Allies. When the manager
/// has no finder under the given name, that action leaves the counter
/// unchanged and appends a warning to the script debug messages. This page
/// does not describe how the threat is computed. Neither Zero Hour nor
/// BFME 1 has the class.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The radius is data-driven at two levels: \c DefaultRadius in the module
/// data and, per placed object, the \c objectThreatFinderRadius map object
/// property. Scripts can query a threat finder only on a placed object
/// that has a name: the finder is registered under the object's name, and
/// an object without one is never registered.
///
/// \see ThreatFinderUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *ThreatFinderUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ThreatFinderUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line, shared with every other object of the
///                    template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see ThreatFinderUpdate,
///      ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Module data for ThreatFinderUpdate: one real-number field,
///        \c DefaultRadius.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. Its field parse
/// function, ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, registers a
/// one-entry table: \c DefaultRadius, a real number. The constructor sets
/// it to 0. ThreatFinderUpdate uses it as the threat finder's radius when
/// the map object does not set \c objectThreatFinderRadius.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The data constructor has the same shape as RadarUpdateModuleData's,
/// whose single field, \c RadarExtendTime (a duration kept as a real
/// number, INI::parseDurationReal), is also a real number set to 0, and in
/// retail both data factories call one shared copy of that constructor.
/// The two classes are distinct all the same: each has its own
/// registration and its own field table.
///
/// \see ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ThreatFinderUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs the data with the constructor it shares with
/// RadarUpdateModuleData (see ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData), then, when
/// \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor default.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see ThreatFinderUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ThreatFinderUpdateModuleData


/// \class ToppleUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_d
/// \brief Module data for the "ToppleUpdate" module: how an object such as
///        a tree falls over, and what is left behind.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// ToppleUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. The module itself is
/// created by ToppleUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, which is not
/// documented here; its registration carries the collide bit as well as
/// update (see the group page). Zero Hour: ToppleUpdate topples its object
/// when something collides with it, and can leave a stump object behind.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table, with the constructor defaults:
/// - \c ToppleFX and \c BounceFX, FX lists looked up by name, none by
///   default;
/// - \c StumpName, text (the name of the object to leave behind), empty by
///   default;
/// - booleans: \c KillWhenFinishedToppling (true by default),
///   \c KillWhenStartToppling, \c KillStumpWhenToppled,
///   \c ToppleLeftOrRightOnly and \c ReorientToppledRubble (false by
///   default);
/// - percentages: \c InitialVelocityPercent (20 percent by default),
///   \c InitialAccelPercent (1 percent) and \c BounceVelocityPercent
///   (20 percent);
/// - \c MinimumToppleSpeed, a real number that must be positive and not
///   zero (INI::parsePositiveNonZeroReal), 0.5 by default.
///
/// The first eleven fields match Zero Hour's ToppleUpdateModuleData table
/// in name and parser; \c MinimumToppleSpeed is not in Zero Hour's table.
///
/// \see ToppleUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *ToppleUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ToppleUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs ToppleUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when
/// \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// ToppleUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see ToppleUpdateModuleData


